===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|sandale|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|sandalium|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|σανδάλιον|lang=grc|sc=Grek|tr=sandalion}}, diminutive of {{term|σάνδαλον|lang=grc|sc=Grek|tr=sandalon||sandal}}, probably ultimately from {{etyl|pal|en}} {{term|𐭰𐭭𐭣𐭫|tr=čandal||sandalwood|lang=pal|sc=Phli}}. Compare New Persian {{term|صندل|tr=sandal||sandal|lang=fa}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ændəl|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A type of open [[shoe]] made up of straps or bands holding a [[sole]] to the foot
# [[sandalwood]]
#* Tennyson
#*: And on the tables every clime and age / Jumbled together: celts and calumets, / Claymore and snow-shoe, toys in lava, fans / Of '''sandal''', amber, ancient rosaries{{...}}

